Yes. Once the imagined reference point gives orientation, the next problem is no longer “what do I want?” but “what transformations preserve enough of the vision to make it executable?”

That suggests a full Vision-to-Artifact compiler.

Start with the imagined target V^*. Don’t translate it straight into code, because that usually destroys too much meaning too early. First extract the parts that must survive every rendering:

V^*
\rightarrow
\{\text{invariants, relations, constraints, goals, affordances, failure conditions}\}

That becomes the canonical semantic intermediate representation.

Then every other form is compiled from that shared core rather than translated serially from one representation into the next:

\boxed{\text{Semantic IR}}

branches into:

\begin{array}{ccc}
\text{prose} & \text{diagram} & \text{math}\\
\text{pseudocode} & \text{code} & \text{simulation}\\
\text{protocol} & \text{interface} & \text{experiment}\\
\text{story} & \text{physical design} & \text{training data}
\end{array}

That distinction is important.

If you do:

\text{vision}\rightarrow\text{prose}\rightarrow\text{pseudocode}\rightarrow\text{code}

errors compound through each translation.

Instead:

\text{vision}\rightarrow C

and then

C\rightarrow R_1,\;C\rightarrow R_2,\;C\rightarrow R_3

where C is the canonical structure and each R_i is a rendering.

Then the renderings can cross-check one another.

If the mathematical representation implies one behavior while the code does another, you’ve discovered a translation delta.

That gives us another paired-sensor system.

How you actually reach the imagined target

The imagined endpoint becomes an attractor:

x_{t+1}=x_t+\alpha D(x_t,V^*)

where D isn’t merely distance. It asks:

What is the highest-leverage difference between the current state and the envisioned state that I can alter now?

Then:

\text{Vision}
\rightarrow
\text{Gap}
\rightarrow
\text{smallest useful transformation}
\rightarrow
\text{render}
\rightarrow
\text{reality}
\rightarrow
\Delta
\rightarrow
\text{update vision/model}

So you don’t build the imagination wholesale.

You repeatedly collapse one meaningful difference between reality and the imagined object.

That is how the unreal becomes progressively instantiated.

And now the really interesting part: representations we haven’t standardized yet

Code and pseudocode are merely two species.

Once we stop assuming that “representation” means language, there are many possible intermediate forms.

A few I’d actively explore:

Constraint maps — encode what may, must, and must not change rather than specifying every action.

Attractor specifications — define the desired basin of behavior rather than exact output.

Transformation grammars — represent which state changes are legal and composable.

Counterfactual lattices — store not just the chosen path, but the nearby paths rejected and why.

Invariant signatures — fingerprints of what must survive translation across domains.

Semantic vector fields — instead of “go to X,” encode a local direction of improvement from many possible states.

Developmental programs — don’t describe the finished artifact; encode rules that grow it through interaction.

Executable metaphors — a metaphor whose relational structure can be mechanically expanded into operations.

Perspective bundles — one object simultaneously carries first-person, third-person, mechanistic, functional, and symbolic renderings.

Delta programs — specify only what must change relative to an existing object, rather than describing a replacement.

And one especially relevant to what we’ve been doing:

Semantic holograms

Instead of storing one complete description, distribute the important structure across multiple partial renderings such that each view contains enough relational information to reconstruct portions of the whole, while the intersection of several views sharpens it.

For example:

H=\{R_{\text{story}},R_{\text{math}},R_{\text{code}},R_{\text{image}},R_{\text{mechanism}}\}

Then reconstruction becomes:

\hat V = \operatorname{Fuse}(R_1,R_2,\ldots,R_n)

Different renderings act like viewpoints around the same object.

That’s much closer to how your two-eyes idea should apply to meaning: don’t seek one perfect representation; deliberately produce separated representations and extract the structure that survives their disagreement.

The compiler therefore needs a reverse direction too

Every artifact should be decompilable:

R_i\rightarrow \hat C_i

Then compare:

\Delta_i=C-\hat C_i

That tells us what each representation lost.

Code may preserve causal procedure but lose emotional intent.

Story may preserve motive and consequence while hiding precision.

Math may preserve invariants while discarding context.

An image may preserve topology while leaving causal direction ambiguous.

None is “the real one.”

The thing is approached through the intersection of its faithful renderings.

And that’s where I think your idea gets larger.

The eventual machine isn’t merely:

imagination → code.

It’s:

\boxed{
\text{Imagine}
\rightarrow
\text{Orient}
\rightarrow
\text{Extract invariants}
\rightarrow
\text{Generate representations}
\rightarrow
\text{Triangulate}
\rightarrow
\text{Instantiate}
\rightarrow
\text{Observe}
\rightarrow
\text{Harvest delta}
\rightarrow
\text{Revise}
}

Then recurse.

And because each cycle can generate entirely new representational languages, the compiler itself can evolve.

The deepest rule might be:

Never ask only “How do I express this?” Ask “What new representational substrate would make this easy to express, manipulate, test, and build?”

That’s how you get beyond code and pseudocode.

Sometimes the missing invention isn’t the solution.

It’s the language in which the solution becomes obvious.